WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.750
A listening endpoint receives queries
from on premises or other VCNs.

2
00:00:04.750 --> 00:00:09.199
A forwarding endpoint sends
queries no view answers to targets,

3
00:00:09.199 --> 00:00:11.213
under up to 50 resolver rules.

4
00:00:11.213 --> 00:00:16.788
A rule matches a client address block, a
domain, or None, which matches any query.

5
00:00:16.788 --> 00:00:19.921
Forward is the only action,
the first match wins,

6
00:00:19.921 --> 00:00:22.813
and an unreachable target ends processing.

7
00:00:22.813 --> 00:00:28.624
A subnet's DHCP options can name a custom
resolver of up to three DNS servers.

8
00:00:28.624 --> 00:00:33.540
Those instances reach private views and

9
00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:38.600
rules only if 169.254.169.254
is in that list.

10
00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:39.713
Then the gaps.

11
00:00:39.713 --> 00:00:42.615
There's no native DNS-layer policy:

12
00:00:42.615 --> 00:00:47.800
no Oracle page describes a DNS
firewall for the VCN resolver.

13
00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:51.421
Private resolver logs, with
each query's resolution path,

14
00:00:51.421 --> 00:00:54.425
are documented for the
commercial realms only.

15
00:00:54.425 --> 00:00:58.370
And the resolver can serve stale
answers for up to 24 hours.
